use std
into cfb.amni

This file is a module with no main (namespace cfb). It reads Microsoft Compound File Binary files (MS-CFB, the container of legacy .doc, .xls, .ppt and .msg files) held in memory: several can be open at once, each named by an integer handle. It copes with version 3 (512-byte sectors) and version 4 (4096-byte sectors), the DIFAT in the header and in extra DIFAT sectors, the FAT, the mini FAT and the mini stream, and it walks the directory tree into one flat list of entries with their parent paths. Every module-level name starts with `cf_` or `CF_`. Every chain it follows is capped by a step count and every sector number is range checked, so a damaged or hostile file can only make a read fail.

```amni
import dm;
```

`CF_FREE` is 0xFFFFFFFF, `CF_END` is 0xFFFFFFFE, `CF_FATSEC` is 0xFFFFFFFD, `CF_DIFSEC` is 0xFFFFFFFC and `CF_MAXSEC` is 0xFFFFFFFA, the highest ordinary sector number (every larger value is special). `CF_NOSTREAM` is 0xFFFFFFFF.

`struct CfFile` has `base int` (where the file's bytes start in `cf_data`), `len int`, `ssz int` (sector size), `mini int` (mini sector size), `cutoff int` (the mini stream cutoff size), `fat0 int` and `nfat int` (its first entry and entry count in `cf_fat`), `mf0 int` and `nmf int` (the same in `cf_mfat`), `ms0 int` and `nms int` (its start and length in `cf_ms`, the mini stream), `e0 int` and `ne int` (its first entry and count in `cf_ents`) and `live bool`.

`struct CfEnt` has `nat int`, `nlen int` (the entry's own name as UTF-8 in `cf_names`), `pat int`, `plen int` (its full path in `cf_names`), `kind int` (1 storage, 2 stream, 5 root), `start int` (first sector) and `size int`.

`struct CfRaw` has `nat int`, `nlen int` (the name as UTF-8 in `cf_names`), `kind int`, `left int`, `right int`, `child int` (directory entry numbers, `CF_NOSTREAM` when none), `start int` and `size int`.

Module state: `var cf_data byte[>..]` (the bytes of every open file one after another), `var cf_fat int[>..]`, `var cf_mfat int[>..]`, `var cf_ms byte[>..]`, `var cf_names byte[>..]`, `var cf_files CfFile[>..]`, `var cf_ents CfEnt[>..]`, and scratch lists used while opening: `var cf_raw CfRaw[>..]`, `var cf_difat int[>..]`, `var cf_work int[>..]` (the walk stack, three ints per item), `var cf_seen byte[>..]` and `var cf_tmp byte[>..]`. A handle is an index into `cf_files`.

`cf_le(s const byte[:], p int, n int) int` reads an unsigned little-endian number of n bytes (1 to 8) at offset p of s; it returns -1 when p is negative, when p plus n passes the end of s, or when the value would not fit in 63 bits.

`cf_rd(f int, p int, n int) int` is `cf_le` on the bytes of file f: it returns -1 when p is negative or p plus n passes that file's length, otherwise the number read from `cf_data` at the file's base plus p.

`cf_sec(f int, s int) int` is the offset inside file f of sector s, which is s plus 1 times the sector size, or -1 when s is negative or above `CF_MAXSEC` or when that offset is at or past the file's length. A file whose last sector is cut short still has that sector.

`cf_eq(a const byte[:], b const byte[:]) bool` compares two names ignoring ASCII case.

`cf_chain_sectors(f int, first int, cap int, out int[>..]&)` appends the sector numbers of the chain that starts at sector first and follows `cf_fat` (the next sector of sector s is entry s of the FAT of file f, taken from `cf_fat` at the file's `fat0` plus s, or the end when s is past `nfat`) to out. It stops at a special sector number, at a sector that `cf_sec` rejects, at a sector that already appeared earlier in this same chain (a loop; keep one byte per sector of the file in a local list to remember them), or after cap sectors, so loops cannot run on.

`cf_sector_bytes(f int, s int, out byte[>..]&, want int) int` appends to out the bytes of sector s of file f: at most want bytes and at most what is left before the end of the file; it returns how many were appended (0 when `cf_sec` rejects s).

`cf_walk(f int)` fills `cf_ents` for the file whose directory entries are in `cf_raw` (the work of `cf_open` after reading the directory). It first pushes the root entry from entry 0 of `cf_raw`: its name, an empty path, kind 5. Then it walks the red-black tree of directory entries, which is the sibling tree hanging from the child of the root, as a flat list in order (left sibling, the entry itself, its children when it is a storage, then right sibling) with an explicit work stack `cf_work` instead of recursion. A work item has a type (0 subtree, 1 emit), a directory entry number, and the index in `cf_ents` (counting from the file's first entry) of the parent. Start with the subtree item of the root's child with parent 0. Popping a subtree item: skip it when the entry number is `CF_NOSTREAM`, at or past the number of raw entries, or already marked in `cf_seen`; otherwise mark it and push, in this order, the subtree item of its right sibling, the emit item of the entry, then the subtree item of its left sibling (so the left one is popped first). Popping an emit item: skip it when the entry's kind is not 1 (storage) or 2 (stream); otherwise push a `CfEnt` whose name is its name, whose path is the parent's path, then a slash, then its name (just its name when the parent is the root), with its kind, start sector and size; when it is a storage whose child is not `CF_NOSTREAM` push the subtree item of that child with this entry as the parent, which will be popped right after. Stop after at most 3 times the number of raw entries pops plus 10, and never push more entries than there are raw entries.

`cf_open(data const byte[:]) int` returns a handle of 0 or more, or -1 when data is not a usable compound file. It needs at least 512 bytes starting with the eight signature bytes 0xD0 0xCF 0x11 0xE0 0xA1 0xB1 0x1A 0xE1. The header fields are little endian: the major version at offset 26 must be 3 or 4, the byte order mark at 28 must be 0xFFFE, the sector shift at 30 must be 9 (512-byte sectors) or 12 (4096-byte sectors), the mini sector shift at 32 must be from 3 to 9 (normally 6, giving 64-byte mini sectors); the number of FAT sectors is at 44, the first directory sector at 48, the mini stream cutoff at 56 (4096 when it is 0), the first mini FAT sector at 60, the mini FAT sector count at 64, the first DIFAT sector at 68, the DIFAT sector count at 72, and 109 DIFAT entries from offset 76. Steps: remember the lengths of `cf_data`, `cf_fat`, `cf_mfat`, `cf_ms`, `cf_names` and `cf_ents`; append data to `cf_data` and push a `CfFile` with live true; clear `cf_raw`, `cf_difat`, `cf_work` and `cf_seen`. The number of sectors in the file is its length divided by the sector size, and every cap below is that number plus 2. Collect the FAT sector numbers in `cf_difat`: every one of the 109 header entries that is at most `CF_MAXSEC`, then follow the extra DIFAT sectors from the first one, at most the DIFAT count (and the cap) of them, each holding sector size divided by 4 minus 1 FAT sector numbers (keep those at most `CF_MAXSEC`) followed by the number of the next DIFAT sector; stop when a sector is rejected by `cf_sec` or is special. Build the FAT: for each collected FAT sector with a valid offset append its sector size divided by 4 little-endian 32-bit numbers (as many as the file holds) to `cf_fat`, and record `fat0` and `nfat` for the file. Read the directory: its sectors are `cf_chain_sectors` from the first directory sector; for each sector read its 128-byte directory entries (a partial entry at the end of the file is ignored): the name length at offset 64 (2 bytes, counting the two zero bytes that end it, ignored when below 2 or above 64), the name itself in UTF-16 little endian converted to UTF-8 with `dm::dm_utf16` into `cf_names`, the type byte at 66, the left sibling at 68, right sibling at 72 and child at 76 (4 bytes each), the start sector at 116 (4 bytes) and the size at 120 (8 bytes; for version 3 files only the low 4 bytes count). A size above 2147483647 is stored as -1. Push a `CfRaw` for each, whatever its type. Fail (undo everything appended to the pools, pop the file, return -1) when there is no directory entry or when entry 0 does not have type 5. Read the mini FAT: sectors `cf_chain_sectors` from the first mini FAT sector, their 32-bit numbers appended to `cf_mfat` (recording `mf0` and `nmf`). Read the mini stream: when the root's size is above 0 and its start sector is valid, read the chain of the root's start sector (cap as above) and append the bytes of those sectors to `cf_ms`, at most the root's size in total, recording `ms0` and `nms`. Then call `cf_walk` and set the file's `e0` and `ne` from `cf_ents`. Return the handle.

`cf_count(h int) int` is the number of entries of handle h, root included, or 0 for a bad or closed handle. Entry 0 is the root entry (name "Root Entry", empty path); the others follow in the flat tree order of `cf_walk`.

`cf_name(h int, i int) const byte[:]` is the entry's own name as UTF-8 (a slice of `cf_names`), empty for a bad handle or index. `cf_path(h int, i int) const byte[:]` is its full path with slashes between the storage names, such as "ObjectPool/Item" (a slice of `cf_names`), empty for the root or a bad index.

`cf_is_stream(h int, i int) bool` is true when the entry exists and has type 2. `cf_size(h int, i int) int` is the size of the entry in bytes (-1 for a bad handle or index; the root's size is the size of the mini stream).

`cf_find(h int, path const byte[:]) int` returns the index of the first entry whose full path equals path ignoring ASCII case (`cf_eq`), after dropping any leading slashes and treating backslashes as slashes in path; an empty path finds the root (0); it returns -1 when nothing matches or the handle is bad.

`cf_read(h int, i int, out byte[>..]&) bool` appends the content of stream i to out and returns true; it returns false (leaving out exactly as it was) for a bad handle, a bad index, an entry that is not a stream, a negative size, a size larger than the whole file's length, or a chain that ends or breaks before the size is reached. A size of 0 succeeds and appends nothing. A stream smaller than the file's mini stream cutoff is read from the mini stream: its chain follows `cf_mfat` from the start mini sector (the next of mini sector s is entry s of the file's part of `cf_mfat`), each mini sector s holding the mini sector size bytes at offset s times that size of the file's part of `cf_ms`, at most (size divided by the mini sector size) plus 2 steps and a mini sector number that repeats within the chain fails the read, the last piece cut to the bytes still needed. Any other stream is read from the FAT: the sectors from `cf_chain_sectors` with a cap of (size divided by the sector size) plus 2, the bytes appended in order and cut to the size (a final sector that the file cuts short counts as short and so fails the read).

`cf_get(h int, path const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` finds the entry with `cf_find`, requires that it is a stream and returns `cf_read` of it; false otherwise.

`cf_close(h int)` marks the handle not live. When no file is live any more it clears every pool (`cf_data`, `cf_fat`, `cf_mfat`, `cf_ms`, `cf_names`, `cf_ents`, `cf_files`).
